Accessibility

You need to make your map as accessible as possible, public sector orgs need to meet their responsibilities under the Equality Act. These are some non-exhaustive ways to make your map more accessible.

Maps are exempt from the Public Sector Accessibility Regulations 2018, but you should still do as much as you can to make your map accessible. 

There’s no comprehensive list of how to make a map ‘fully accessible’. Test with your users to identify which areas you need to prioritise.

Introducing and explaining the map to users: 
Show one thing per map - don’t try to make one map do too much 
Have a clear explanation of what the map is and how to use it on the surrounding page
Use plain English for all content on the map 

Making the map itself more accessible:
Interacting with the maps, using a keyboard - Make sure your map is keyboard accessible [moved from section above?] Use standard controls, and allow small and large step navigation
Colours and colour contrast - colours should contrast highly enough with the basemap and with each other
Accessibility - Colour Vision Deficiency, min font size
ARIA and screen readers
Especially when using sequential colouring to visualise data, ensure there is a query function allows the user to check the data through clicking or hovering
How to describe the map to meet accessibility standards eg some good examples of services with maps
Giving users choice about how to display the map e.g. being able to choose their preferred basemap (third party tools like Esri do this)
Use of transparencies and order of layering
OS approach to produce 3 versions that are tuned to help with different visual impairments eg use CSS - people can choose the view that suits them
Where vector tiles allow use solid colour for map layers above a basemap, and bring basemap taxonomy above any solid data layers to show map details beneath
Different stylesheets for different needs (colour vision, low/high contrast etc)
