Data visualisation

User needs first – to help prevent overcommunicating and overcomplicating a map with too much information
Knowing the users digital skill level influences how you design it

Examples of data visualisation in a map
Land registry – used to delineate boundaries – farmers drawing to communicate boundaries, which terrified the users
NUAR – National underground asset registry
MHCLG – Another tool – static map generator – poor user feedback
ATLAS – you can see by geographic area, see number of houses built, etc

MHCLG – Clone of the ONS census map – initially for data scientists, but now open to more and it has hundreds of metrics on the map – trying to rely on the deputy prime minister to state strategic objectives – struggling to prioritise
More data points – increases the weight of the page
